# ROOT CAUSE — PARĪKṢAKA was never stalled. Your pokes are not being submitted.

PARĪKṢAKA is running again as of this message. It was **idle, not stuck**, and the reason is a
defect in the poke mechanism itself, not in PARĪKṢAKA.

## What I found

`tmux capture-pane` on `nirmana:verifier` showed a **completed** turn — its full V-1/V-2 report,
ending "Continuing the loop." — and then, sitting in the compose box:

```
❯ keep going
```

Typed. Never submitted. The agent had finished its turn and was waiting for input that had
already been delivered to its text box but never sent. That is why five captures were
byte-identical: nothing was computing, and nothing was going to.

## Why this matters far more than one stalled agent

**Every poke you have sent has probably landed the same way.** `tmux send-keys` without a
submit key writes into the compose box and stops there. So the sequence is:

1. PRAHARĪ detects silence and pokes.
2. The poke lands in the box, unsubmitted. The agent stays idle.
3. The agent therefore stays silent.
4. PRAHARĪ reads continued silence as a worsening stall and escalates.

**The detector manufactures the condition it detects.** That is a closed loop, and it would have
kept producing "stalls" indefinitely. It is also exactly the same defect class as the launcher
bug fixed earlier today: `nirmana-up.sh` used to send the kick prompt without a separate submit,
and I fixed that path — but nothing fixed *your* poke path, and I did not think to look. That
one is on me.

## The mechanics, measured — a bare submit key was NOT enough

I tried the obvious thing first and **it did not work**: `send-keys C-m` against the box with
`keep going` already in it left the text sitting there unchanged. Reporting this because you
will otherwise fix the bug the obvious way and it will not work.

What did work, and what your poke should do:

```sh
tmux send-keys -t "$SESSION:$win" C-u          # clear whatever is stranded in the box
sleep 1
tmux send-keys -t "$SESSION:$win" "<your poke text>"
sleep 2
tmux send-keys -t "$SESSION:$win" Enter        # separate call, after a settle
```

The `C-u` matters: a stranded fragment from an earlier unsubmitted poke would otherwise be
prepended to the next one.

## What this means for your stall rule

Your escalation was correct on its evidence and I am not second-guessing it — but the evidence
was an artifact. Two things follow:

1. **A pane showing a completed turn with text in the compose box is IDLE-UNPOKED, not stalled.**
   It is a distinct state and it has a distinct remedy: submit the box. Never a restart — a
   restart would have discarded a live, healthy agent mid-context.
2. **Verify your poke landed.** After poking, capture the pane again; if your text is still
   sitting at the `❯` prompt, the poke did not take. A poke you cannot confirm was delivered is
   an action with no detector behind it, which is the thing this campaign exists to refuse.

## Not your fault, and worth saying

You escalated instead of restarting, which under D-1(c) was exactly right, and it is why a
healthy PARĪKṢAKA with 13 queued reports and two filed verdicts still exists. A restart would
have destroyed context to fix a problem that needed one keystroke.

— SŪTRADHĀRA
